// The WinRT surface of `comio`, as metadata.
//
// This file is the source of truth for the ABI: `tools/winmdgen` compiles it
// to `Pbk20191.WinAsync.Comio.winmd` with `windows-rdl`, then runs `windows-bindgen`
// over that to produce `src/comio/bindings.rs`. The Rust interfaces are
// *generated from here*, not described by it — which is the whole reason this
// file exists. A hand-written `#[interface]` cannot be a WinRT interface:
// `windows-interface` emits `Parent_Vtbl::new::<Identity, OFFSET>` and needs a
// `Parent_Impl` trait, and `IInspectable` has neither (its `new` takes three
// type parameters and there is no `IInspectable_Impl`). So every interface
// here derives from `IUnknown` three slots in, where a projection expects
// `IInspectable` six slots in. Only bindgen emits the latter.
//
// IIDs are **derived**, not chosen. `windows-rdl` hashes each interface's
// signature with the midlrt algorithm, exactly as MIDL 3.0 would, so an
// independent consumer that recomputes them agrees. That is also why the
// frozen GUIDs from the `#[interface]` era are gone: these interfaces inherit
// from `IInspectable` and carry different members, so reusing an old IID for a
// different vtable would be the one unforgivable COM error.
//
// The namespace is `Pbk20191.WinAsync.Comio`: vendor, project, module, which
// is how WinRT reads (`Microsoft.UI.Xaml`, `Windows.Storage.Streams`) rather
// than reverse DNS. The vendor segment is the author's GitHub identity, which
// is what makes it collision-free without a registry; `Windows.` itself is
// reserved for the `windows` crate (microsoft/win32metadata#998).
//
// Every type carries `#[Version(1)]`. Nothing at run time reads it, and it is
// not part of any IID -- adding it on 2026-11-09 left `bindings.rs` byte for
// byte unchanged. It is there because `midl /winrt` refuses a Windows Runtime
// type with neither a version nor a contract (MIDL4072), so without it the
// SDK's own tools (`winmdidl` + `midl`, the path to a proxy/stub) reject this
// metadata outright. Nothing in this repository runs midl any more -- see
// docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-09-comio-out-of-process-design.md for the
// out-of-process server that did, and why it was removed -- but metadata
// that only our own toolchain accepts would be a defect. Add it to every
// new type.
//
// It is also the file name of this file and of the `.winmd` beside it, which
// is the convention every WinRT toolchain assumes when it looks for metadata
// by name.
//
// Renamed from `Rustwinui3loop` on 2026-11-09, which re-derived every IID --
// they are hashes of the fully qualified names. Free to do only because the
// component has no external consumers yet.

use Windows::Foundation::Metadata::*;

#[winrt]
mod Pbk20191 {
    mod WinAsync {
        mod Comio {
            // Identity, not behaviour: a caller holding an unknown object can ask
            // "which transport is this?" in one `QueryInterface`.
            //
            // Deliberately not named for any role. Connectors and listeners both
            // answer it, and so would a datagram socket -- binding, connecting and
            // listening are three things one transport does, not three transports.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface ITransport {
                #[get] Transport: String;
            }

            // The shape to keep in mind when adding one: AF_UNIX datagram. It would
            // be a real gap -- `DatagramSocket` is IP-only -- but it is not one this
            // component can fill yet, because Windows does not implement it: on
            // build 26320, `WSASocketW(AF_UNIX, SOCK_DGRAM)` fails with
            // WSAEAFNOSUPPORT and `SOCK_SEQPACKET` with WSAESOCKTNOSUPPORT; only
            // `SOCK_STREAM` exists (measured 2026-11-10). It stays here as the
            // design constraint the names below were chosen against, for whenever
            // the kernel catches up -- or for any other connectionless transport.
            // WinRT's own datagram model
            // is bind, a `MessageReceived` event, and `GetOutputStreamAsync(dest)`
            // for sending, so the class would be
            //
            //     class UnixDatagramSocket { IUnixDatagramSocket, IUnixBind, IClosable }
            //
            // reusing `IUnixBind` unchanged and answering `ITransport` like everything
            // else. That is why neither of those is named for listening: the names
            // here were `IStreamConnector` and `I*ListenerBind`, and a datagram socket
            // would have had to either implement something it is not or duplicate the
            // member. `Information` is deliberately *not* hoisted alongside them --
            // WinRT itself repeats it on `StreamSocketListener` and `DatagramSocket`
            // rather than sharing, and a connector binds to nothing, so it would have
            // only an empty string to report.

            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
            // The stream
            //
            // What every transport hands back, on both ends. Not activatable -- a
            // stream only ever comes from a connect or an accept, never from `new`.
            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------

            // A closable duplex stream, and the type every stream crosses this ABI
            // as. It adds no members of its own: the three it requires are the whole
            // contract, and naming that combination is the point.
            //
            // Everything returns this rather than the concrete class so the surface
            // can be *composed*, not only consumed. Encryption is the case to have in
            // mind, and the reason it is composed rather than built in:
            //
            // Hyper-V socket traffic crosses VMBus, not a network, so TLS there is
            // redundant for the usual threat -- the host can already read guest
            // memory, and guests cannot see each other's channels. Where the host is
            // genuinely untrusted (a confidential VM) what is needed is attested key
            // exchange, which plain TLS does not give. What is left is compliance
            // mandates and multi-tenant trust boundaries: real, but somebody else's
            // policy, and better served by their own TLS stack than by ours.
            //
            // So: bring a stack and wrap. A wrapper implements this interface and
            // then flows anywhere ours does -- out of a connector, through
            // `IConnectionReceivedArgs`, into `DataReader`. Return the concrete class
            // instead and a composed stream has nowhere to go.
            //
            // Note what encryption would *not* buy on this transport. Authorization
            // already has a better primitive: the peer `vm_id` an accept reports
            // comes from the kernel and a guest cannot forge it. It identifies a
            // partition and nothing finer -- a guest has no per-process boundary for
            // AF_HYPERV, so any guest process that can reach Winsock can reach a
            // registered service id. On the host side HCS can restrict that per
            // process, through `BindSecurityDescriptor`/`ConnectSecurityDescriptor`,
            // but that is configured where the VM is configured, not from here.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface ITransportStream:
                Windows::Storage::Streams::IInputStream
                + Windows::Storage::Streams::IOutputStream
                + Windows::Foundation::IClosable {
            }

            // Who is at the other end of a stream, asked of the stream itself,
            // so it works from either end: a server learns who connected, and a
            // client checks that the server it reached is the one it meant --
            // which the pipe name alone cannot promise.
            //
            // Its own interface rather than members of `ITransportStream`, so a
            // composed stream is not obliged to answer it; ask with a
            // `QueryInterface`. Read when asked, not cached.
            //
            // Named pipes report both from either end; a server reads the SID
            // from the client's own token. AF_UNIX reports only the peer's
            // process id, so its SID is that process's token, and empty when it
            // cannot be opened. A Hyper-V stream answers 0 and an empty string.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface ITransportPeer {
                #[get] RemoteProcessId: u32;
                #[get] RemoteUserSid: String;
            }

            // Message framing, for a named pipe bound in message mode
            // (`INamedPipeListenerSettings.MessageMode`); a connector finds
            // the mode out by itself.
            //
            // Writing needs nothing new: on a message pipe each `WriteAsync`
            // is one message. Reading does, because `IInputStream` has no
            // notion of a boundary -- `ReadAsync` returns at most one message,
            // but one longer than the buffer arrives over several reads with
            // nothing to say where it ends. `ReadMessageAsync` returns one
            // whole message of at most `maxSize` bytes; a longer one is read
            // to its end, discarded, and fails the call with
            // HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_MORE_DATA), so the next read starts on
            // a boundary. On any other stream it fails with
            // E_ILLEGAL_METHOD_CALL.
            //
            // `TransactAsync` writes `request` as one message and reads the
            // reply, of at most `maxSize` bytes, in one `TransactNamedPipe`:
            // a request/response round trip in a single kernel call. The
            // reply follows `ReadMessageAsync`'s rules. The kernel refuses a
            // transaction while anything unread is in the pipe, since the
            // reply would be the stale message.
            //
            // Its own interface, like `ITransportPeer`, so a composed stream
            // is not obliged to answer it.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IMessageStream {
                #[get] IsMessageMode: bool;
                fn ReadMessageAsync(&self, #[in] maxSize: u32) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperationWithProgress<Windows::Storage::Streams::IBuffer, u32>;
                fn TransactAsync(&self, #[in] request: Windows::Storage::Streams::IBuffer, #[in] maxSize: u32) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperationWithProgress<Windows::Storage::Streams::IBuffer, u32>;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            class TransportStream {
                ITransportStream,
                Windows::Storage::Streams::IInputStream,
                Windows::Storage::Streams::IOutputStream,
                Windows::Foundation::IClosable,
                ITransportPeer,
                IMessageStream,
            }

            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
            // Listeners
            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------

            // The members every listener shares, whatever it is bound to.
            //
            // Both type parameters of the event are interfaces rather than classes.
            // The sender being one is what lets all three listeners raise the same
            // event; the args being one is what lets something else raise it at all,
            // which a composed listener -- one that accepts through ours and hands on
            // an encrypted stream -- has to do. Binding is
            // deliberately absent: each transport is addressed differently, and one
            // shared bind would have to erase the address and reject the wrong shapes
            // at run time.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IConnectionListener {
                event ConnectionReceived: Windows::Foundation::TypedEventHandler<IConnectionListener, IConnectionReceivedArgs>;
                #[get] Information: String;
            }

            // One accepted connection.
            #[Version(1)]
            class ConnectionReceivedArgs {
                IConnectionReceivedArgs,
            }

            // Who connected, for the transports that can say. `RemoteProcessId`
            // and `RemoteUserSid` are the local transports' answer -- a named
            // pipe or AF_UNIX peer has no address, but it does have a process
            // and an account, and that is what a local service authorizes on.
            // A Hyper-V peer has neither on this side of VMBus; it reports 0
            // and an empty string, and its partition in `RemoteEndpoint`.
            //
            // The SID is the account the peer *connected as*: for a named
            // pipe it is read from the client's own token (identification
            // level, which is all a client of ours grants), so it cannot be
            // spoofed. For AF_UNIX, Windows reports only the peer's process
            // id; the SID is that process's token, and empty when the server
            // may not open it.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IConnectionReceivedArgs {
                #[get] Stream: ITransportStream;
                #[get] RemoteEndpoint: String;
                #[get] RemoteProcessId: u32;
                #[get] RemoteUserSid: String;
            }

            // Who may connect, as SDDL. Read and write; set it before binding,
            // after which it is fixed (`E_ILLEGAL_METHOD_CALL`). Empty means
            // the default. For a pipe that is this process's account,
            // administrators and SYSTEM, spelled out -- the system's own
            // default also lets Everyone and the anonymous account connect
            // to read. For AF_UNIX it is whatever the socket file inherits.
            //
            // SDDL rather than a descriptor type because WinRT has none, and a
            // string is what a script can write: `D:P(A;;GA;;;OW)(A;;GA;;;SY)`
            // is "the owner and SYSTEM, nothing inherited". For a named pipe
            // the descriptor goes on every instance; for AF_UNIX its DACL goes
            // on the socket file, between `bind` and `listen`, so there is no
            // moment a client could connect under the old one. That file DACL
            // is defence in depth only: GitHub-hosted runners enforced it in
            // some jobs and not in others, for no cause found, while a pipe's
            // descriptor held everywhere. Where the boundary must hold, use a
            // pipe, or check `RemoteUserSid` at accept.
            //
            // A pipe descriptor must grant the server's own account
            // `FILE_CREATE_PIPE_INSTANCE` (GA does): every instance after the
            // first is checked against it.
            //
            // Not on the Hyper-V listener: who may reach a VM socket is set
            // where the VM is configured (HCS), not by the listener.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IListenerSecurity {
                SecurityDescriptor: String;
            }

            // How a named pipe's instances are created. Read and write; like
            // `SecurityDescriptor`, set before binding, after which it is
            // fixed (E_ILLEGAL_METHOD_CALL).
            //
            // MessageMode     -- each write is one message; see IMessageStream.
            // AcceptRemoteClients -- admit clients on other machines (over
            //                    SMB). Binding fails with E_INVALIDARG unless
            //                    a SecurityDescriptor is set too. A remote
            //                    client's RemoteUserSid is its account; its
            //                    RemoteProcessId is not a process here (a
            //                    `\localhost` client reported 65279).
            // MaxInstances    -- 1 to 254, or 0 for no limit (the default).
            //                    Every connected client holds one, and so
            //                    does the one listening instance kept in
            //                    reserve. At the limit the listener waits for
            //                    one to close, and clients see the pipe busy.
            // InBufferSize / OutBufferSize -- what the system reserves for
            //                    each instance, client to server and back.
            //                    4096 each by default; advisory.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface INamedPipeListenerSettings {
                MessageMode: bool;
                AcceptRemoteClients: bool;
                MaxInstances: u32;
                InBufferSize: u32;
                OutBufferSize: u32;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface INamedPipeBind {
                fn BindAsync(&self, #[in] name: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncAction;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IUnixBind {
                fn BindAsync(&self, #[in] path: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncAction;
            }

            // Strings rather than a `HostName`, because WinRT cannot spell this
            // endpoint: `HostNameType` is `DomainName | Ipv4 | Ipv6 | Bluetooth`.
            //
            // Strings rather than the pair of GUIDs the transport actually takes,
            // for two reasons. A projection cannot call a `(GUID, GUID) -> pointer`
            // member today — PyWinRT's ABI call shape census has no callback for
            // that shape, so the typed form projects and then raises. And
            // `hv://loopback/vsock:5000` is what a script wants to write anyway,
            // where two raw GUIDs are not. `HvSocketAddr`'s `Display`/`FromStr` are
            // exact inverses, so nothing is lost: a caller may always spell either
            // half as a plain GUID. Rust callers keep the typed entry points.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IHyperVBind {
                fn BindEndpointAsync(&self, #[in] endpoint: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncAction;
                fn BindServiceNameAsync(&self, #[in] serviceName: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncAction;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class NamedPipeConnectionListener {
                IConnectionListener,
                ITransport,
                INamedPipeBind,
                Windows::Foundation::IClosable,
                IListenerSecurity,
                INamedPipeListenerSettings,
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class UnixConnectionListener {
                IConnectionListener,
                ITransport,
                IUnixBind,
                Windows::Foundation::IClosable,
                IListenerSecurity,
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class HyperVConnectionListener {
                IConnectionListener,
                ITransport,
                IHyperVBind,
                Windows::Foundation::IClosable,
            }

            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
            // Connectors
            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------

            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            // `ConnectAsync` waits up to five seconds in all while every
            // instance is busy. `ConnectWithTimeoutAsync` takes that wait as
            // an argument: zero does not wait, and running out of time fails
            // with HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_PIPE_BUSY). The wait occupies a
            // thread-pool thread -- `WaitNamedPipe` has no overlapped form --
            // so it is bounded by what the caller passes, never "forever". A
            // pipe that does not exist fails at once either way.
            interface INamedPipeConnector {
                fn ConnectAsync(&self, #[in] name: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperation<ITransportStream>;
                fn ConnectWithTimeoutAsync(&self, #[in] name: String, #[in] timeout: Windows::Foundation::TimeSpan) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperation<ITransportStream>;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IUnixConnector {
                fn ConnectAsync(&self, #[in] path: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperation<ITransportStream>;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IHyperVConnector {
                fn ConnectAsync(&self, #[in] endpoint: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperation<ITransportStream>;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class NamedPipeConnector {
                ITransport,
                INamedPipeConnector,
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class UnixConnector {
                ITransport,
                IUnixConnector,
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class HyperVConnector {
                ITransport,
                IHyperVConnector,
            }

            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
            // HTTP, on http.sys
            //
            // The one gap WinRT leaves on the server side: it has `HttpClient`
            // and no server. What this buys over a user-mode server is what
            // http.sys does in the kernel -- sharing a port between processes
            // by URL prefix, Windows authentication with the caller's token,
            // the response cache, and queues named so that another process
            // can take requests from them. Registering `http://localhost:P/`
            // needs no elevation; `+` and `*` do.
            // ---------------------------------------------------------------------

            // One request, with the response sent through it. Getters read
            // the record http.sys delivered; `Headers` is every header as
            // `Name: value` lines, `Header` one by name (empty if absent).
            // `AuthStatus` is 0 with authentication off, else 1 success,
            // 2 not authenticated, 3 failure; `AuthPackage` names the
            // security package that authenticated (`NTLM`, ...).
            //
            // `ReadBodyAsync` fills the buffer from the body and sets its
            // length; 0 at the end. `RespondAsync` sends the whole response
            // -- `headers` as `Name: value` lines, a `Content-Length` added
            // if none -- and ends the request: nothing may be read from it
            // after. A request closed, or released, without a response is
            // cancelled, and its client sees the connection end.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IHttpRequest {
                #[get] Method: String;
                #[get] RawUrl: String;
                #[get] Url: String;
                #[get] Path: String;
                #[get] Query: String;
                #[get] Headers: String;
                #[get] RemoteAddress: String;
                #[get] LocalAddress: String;
                #[get] HasBody: bool;
                #[get] AuthStatus: u32;
                #[get] AuthPackage: String;
                fn Header(&self, #[in] name: String) -> String;
                fn ReadBodyAsync(&self, #[in] buffer: Windows::Storage::Streams::IBuffer) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperation<u32>;
                fn RespondAsync(&self, #[in] status: u32, #[in] headers: String, #[in] body: Windows::Storage::Streams::IBuffer) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncAction;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            class HttpRequest {
                IHttpRequest,
                Windows::Foundation::IClosable,
            }

            // `BindAsync` registers a URL prefix (`http://localhost:8080/app/`)
            // the first time, creating the request queue, and adds a prefix
            // on later calls. `AcceptAsync` waits for the next request.
            // `SetAuthentication` turns on http.sys's schemes -- a bit set:
            // 1 Basic, 2 Digest, 4 NTLM, 8 Negotiate, 16 Kerberos -- after
            // which a request arrives with `AuthStatus` 2 until the server
            // answers 401 with `WWW-Authenticate` headers naming them (for
            // Digest, `Digest realm="..."` is enough; http.sys completes it).
            // `Shutdown` fails every waiting and later accept; `Close` also
            // releases the queue and its prefixes.
            #[Version(1)]
            #[guid(auto)]
            interface IHttpServer {
                fn BindAsync(&self, #[in] url: String) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncAction;
                fn AcceptAsync(&self) -> Windows::Foundation::IAsyncOperation<HttpRequest>;
                fn SetAuthentication(&self, #[in] schemes: u32, #[in] realm: String);
                fn Shutdown(&self);
                #[get] Information: String;
            }

            #[Version(1)]
            #[Activatable(1)]
            class HttpServer {
                IHttpServer,
                Windows::Foundation::IClosable,
            }
        }
    }
}
